Apple previously announced that the <a href="https://discussions.apple.com/">Apple Support Community</a> discussion forums for user-to-user tech support will be permanently closing this Friday, November 20. The forums have existed in some form for 28 years. It is the end of an era.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/Apple-Support-Community.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076860" /><br/>
It is our understanding that the forums will eventually be removed entirely, meaning that existing posts will no longer be visible. However, it is not clear if that will happen immediately on November 20 or at a later date.<br/>
<br/>
Already, the Apple Support Community forums are in a read-only state, with new posts no longer being accepted. The <a href="https://discussions.apple.com/thread/256392294?answerId=262201996022&sortBy=rank#262201996022">final message</a> posted to the site appears to have been submitted on November 10 at 5:49 p.m. Pacific Time. On the English forums, this change happened abruptly, leaving members with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/11/apple-support-community-deserved-a-proper-goodbye/">no chance to post farewell messages</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Apple's notice to visitors:<blockquote>The Apple Support Community will permanently close on November 20, 2026. Starting today, the site will no longer accept new posts and is available in a read-only state.<br/>
<br/>
You can continue to find trusted guidance and support on the official <a href="https://support.apple.com/">Apple Support site</a>. <br/>
<br/>
We want to thank the community members who have contributed their time and expertise to help others with their Apple products.</blockquote>The forums have a 28-year history. Apple offered an online discussion forum for user-to-user tech support since 1998, when it launched the Apple Tech Exchange. The forums were renamed to Apple Discussions in 2000, and then to the Apple Support Communities in 2011, before taking on their current Apple Support Community name in 2022.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/Screenshot-2026-11-11-at-10.44.42-AM.png" alt="" width="2444" height="1604" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076953" /><br/>
Apple informed <em>MacRumors</em> that it decided to sunset the Apple Support Community after a careful evaluation of strategy and customer needs. <br/>
<br/>
